%N A397143 Numbers k such that k times its decimal digit reversal contains all ten distinct decimal digits.
%C A397143 The sequence consists of the indices n for which A401895(n) = 10.
%C A397143 The number of terms less than 10^k for k = 4, 5, 6 is 0, 109, 5613, respectively.
%C A397143 Unlike A178929, which requires every decimal digit to occur exactly once, this sequence permits repeated digits. Consequently, A178929 is a finite subsequence of this infinite sequence.
%C A397143 If k is a term and is not divisible by 10, then its decimal digit reversal is also a term.
%C A397143 The sequence is infinite: if k is a term, then 10^m*k is also a term for every m >= 0.
%e A397143 a(1) = 14979 because 14979*97941 = 1467058239, which contains all ten decimal digits.
%e A397143 a(2) = 19167 because 19167*76191 = 1460352897, which contains all ten decimal digits.
%e A397143 The reversal of 14979 is 97941, which is also a term, namely a(104).
%o A397143 (Python)
%o A397143 def is_ok(n):
%o A397143     return len(set(str(n * int(str(n)[::-1])))) == 10
%o A397143 (PARI)
%o A397143 is_ok(n) = #Set(digits(n*fromdigits(Vecrev(digits(n))))) == 10;
%Y A397143 Cf. A004086, A043537, A061205, A178929, A401895.
